$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "814d60a6-06cf-5b50-94e2-99e3281e5351" :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **Y.** $Y(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **H.** $H(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ is a scalar in $\\mathbb R$. A denominator must be nonzero. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{H}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid x+1\\ne0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **N.** $N(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its three standard-basis coefficients give \n$$\nN(x,y,z)=\\langle \\sin(x), \\cos(y), \\cot(z)\\rangle.\n$$\nExclude the poles of the trigonometric component. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{N}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid z\\ne k\\pi,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $N$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4fc49c6-796c-5a48-ab32-5542c2c5404b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.typ